Fix worldbook toggle doing nothing: write the array directly

The toggle relied on the tavern's own onWorldInfoChange(), which looks a
world up like this (world-info.js lines 5657/5660):

    text.trim().toLowerCase().split(',')
    getWIElement(worldName)

and getWIElement (line 2090) matches each option's text against the name.
Any of these breaks the whole chain silently:
  - the #world_info select is not populated yet (it only fills in once the
    World Info panel has been opened, see updateWorldInfoList)
  - the name contains a comma and gets split apart
  - the name does not match an option

Symptom: clicking the switch did nothing, only a "could not change" toast.

Now we write selected_world_info directly. It is declared as
`export let selected_world_info = []` (line 66) and line 85 does
Object.assign(world_info, { globalSelect: selected_world_info }), so the
exported array IS the live data, not a copy. Imported bindings cannot be
reassigned, but the array contents can be mutated in place -- so push/splice
works and reaches exactly the data the tavern uses.

After writing we then save settings, emit WORLDINFO_SETTINGS_UPDATED, and
best-effort align the select's selected props so an already-open World Info
panel shows the change. Those steps are no longer required for the toggle
to work.

Also:
- drop the now-unused onWorldInfoChange import
- use a while loop when removing so duplicate entries are all cleared
- guard against double-adding the same name
- the failure toast now points at the console instead of claiming the
  feature is unsupported
- _selftest_toggle.mjs rewritten for the new implementation, 23 assertions
  (adds comma-in-name on/off, duplicate cleanup, double-add guard, and
  that the select alignment is called)
- _verify_load.mjs stub: selected_world_info is now `export let`,
  onWorldInfoChange stub removed

Verified: syntax OK, 15 pairing + 23 toggle + 10 ps1 assertions all pass,
and the real-load simulation still loads with the init hook callable.
